@value:
---------
We can inject the value of a field from application properties or application.yml files into your bean.

By using this we can ensure dynamic configuration.

@value("${application.something.something}").

Drawbacks of @Value:
-------------------
1. No Type safety(Everything is String by default).
2. Values scattered across the Application.
3. No grouping of related settings. For nested or grouping it becomes messy and repetative.
4. Refactoring becomes risky.
5. harder to test.
6. Harder to maintain the consistency across the Environments.


@ConfigurationProperties:
-------------------------
@ConfigurationProperties in Spring Boot is a powerful annotation used for binding external configuration (from 
application.properties or application.yml) to Java objects in a type-safe way.

It allows you to map a group of related properties into a POJO (Plain Old Java Object).
Instead of using multiple @Value annotations, you can bind all properties under a common prefix to a single class.

Key Benefits


1. Type safety
“If the config type mismatches, Spring fails at startup — not at runtime.”


2. Easier testing

You can simply create an instance of the config class in unit tests.
No Spring needed.


3. Grouping related config

Your properties are logically organized.
One bean for one feature.


Supports nested properties

Great for microservices with complex config.


